# Target C — C88 staircase separator-budget frontier

**LEVEL I STATUS: STILL OPEN.**

Full proof: [target_c_staircase_separator_budget_2026-09-01.md](../../reports/target_c_staircase_separator_budget_2026-09-01.md).

C87 forces the canonical chain prefix graph to be an antitone tree with exactly `D`
simple interval edges. C88 audits the equality case of the C87 count and obtains an
exact staircase parametrization. In particular the total simple-edge chain span is
shape-independent:

`sum_e |I_e| = D(D+1)/2`,

so

`sum_e (D-|I_e|)=D(D-1)/2`.

For any active diameter pair `h`, exact train coordinates give

`r_h=sum_tau d_(h,tau)u_tau`.

The nonzero summands are unit vectors and `|r_h|=D`, hence the number `k_h` of trains
separating the endpoints satisfies `k_h>=D`. If the C83 chain interval has length
`ell_h`, at least `D-ell_h` separating trains lie outside the `D`-track chain atom.

C87 supplies at least two actual diameter pairs over every simple tree edge. Selecting
one of each orientation gives the exact lower budget

`sum_(selected h) # {nonchain trains separating h} >= D(D-1)`.

Thus the paired staircase has a quadratic mandatory incidence with the other train
directions.

In the asymptotic pure-rhombic branch, `q/T>=1/2-o(1)` and `q<=pi D+o(D)` give
`T<=2pi D+o(D)`. Therefore some non-chain train separates at least

`(1/(2pi-1)+o(1))D = (0.189279...+o(1))D`

selected active diameter pairs.

The next exact target is a capacity theorem for this macroscopically loaded secondary
train cut. Its crossing diameter subgraph is bipartite and hence acyclic because
planar diameter cycles are odd; the ordered-thrackle reduction should force a
caterpillar-type structure that must be reconciled with C87's paired staircase.